[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Accessibility Implementation in SwiftUI"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building inclusive apps is essential for reaching all users. SwiftUI provides powerful, declarative modifiers that make implementing Dynamic Type, VoiceOver, and accessibility labels straightforward and efficient."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Supporting Dynamic Type"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dynamic Type allows users to scale text to their preferred reading size. SwiftUI text elements scale automatically when using standard system fonts, but custom designs require careful layout consideration to avoid clipping."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Text("Welcome Back!")
    .font(.system(.body, design: .rounded))
    .lineLimit(nil)
    .minimumScaleFactor(0.8)"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Enhancing VoiceOver with Labels and Hints"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Screen readers rely on text descriptions to convey UI context. By default, SwiftUI aggregates adjacent text views, but complex components like custom buttons require explicit accessibility labels and hints."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"Button(action: {
    // Action code here
}) {
    Image(systemName: "heart.fill")
        .foregroundColor(.red)
}
.accessibilityLabel("Like Post")
.accessibilityHint("Double tap to add this item to your favorites")"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Grouping and Excluding Elements"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes multiple visual elements form a single logical concept. Use accessibility clustering to group elements together, or hide decorative graphics entirely to reduce VoiceOver clutter."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"HStack {
    Image(systemName: "star.fill")
        .accessibilityHidden(true)
    Text("4.8 Rating")
}
.accessibilityElement(hiding: false)
.accessibilityLabel("Rating: 4.8 out of 5 stars")"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for SwiftUI Accessibility"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always test your app using the Accessibility Inspector in Xcode."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure interactive elements meet the minimum touch target size of 44x44 points."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid using color as the only indicator of state changes by combining it with text or icons."}]
